1. Simple Factory

What is it?
A Simple Factory is a class or method that creates objects based on an input parameter.
Instead of creating objects directly, the client asks the factory to create them.

Why do we need it?
Imagine you have multiple payment types. Without a factory, you might write new CardPayment() and new PayPalPayment() in many places. If the creation logic changes, you may need to update all those places.
A Simple Factory centralizes this logic.

The factory decides which implementation to create.
Advantages: Easy to understand, centralizes creation logic, and reduces direct dependencies in the client.
Disadvantage: Every time we add a new payment type, we usually need to modify the factory. This can conflict with the Open/Closed Principle.

GOTO: PaymentFactory.cs => Main.cs / PaymentFactory.java => Main.java

